      "id": "exam/edexcel-9fm0-02-2020-october/q5",
      "question": "<ol class=\"parts\"><li><div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"y=\\tan^{-1}x\" data-display=\"true\"></div>Assuming the derivative of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\tan x\"></span>, prove that<div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\frac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dx}=\\frac{1}{1+x^2}\" data-display=\"true\"></div><span class=\"marks\">(3)</span></li></ol><div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\mathrm f(x)=x\\tan^{-1}4x\" data-display=\"true\"></div><ol class=\"parts\" start=\"2\"><li>Show that<div class=\"equation\" data-math=\"\\int\\mathrm f(x)\\,\\mathrm dx=Ax^2\\tan^{-1}4x+Bx+C\\tan^{-1}4x+k\" data-display=\"true\"></div>where <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"k\"></span> is an arbitrary constant and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"A\"></span>, <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"B\"></span> and <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"C\"></span> are constants to be determined. <span class=\"marks\">(5)</span></li><li>Hence find, in exact form, the mean value of <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\mathrm f(x)\"></span> over the interval <span class=\"inline-math\" data-math=\"\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{4}\\right]\"></span> <span class=\"marks\">(2)</span></li></ol>",
